[Simplification] The latch isn't needed. `GarbageCollectionHandler` is already a `Consumer<RecordedEvent>`, so it can be passed straight to `onEvent`, and the test can wait for the metric itself. `InMemoryMetricReader` is cumulative by default, so polling doesn't break the assertions that follow. This removes the latch, the wrapper lambda and the nested timed wait. Separately, drop the `CountDownLatch` and `MILLISECONDS` imports and the `throws InterruptedException` clause.

```suggestion
      try (RecordingStream stream = new RecordingStream()) {
        stream.enable(handler.getEventName());
        stream.onEvent(handler.getEventName(), handler);
        stream.startAsync();
        await()
            .atMost(Duration.ofSeconds(30))
            .until(() -> {
              System.gc();
              return !reader.collectAllMetrics().isEmpty();
            });
```

